{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794006823,"nonce":"SuC2eZQR1+kYpIK9iubi3Akjabk=","message":"Baseline on the shipped v0.1.16 Linux binary (no new code), NUC, hybrid, taskset 0,2,4,6, load 4.3 at start (not quiet), the reproducer above, 40 s: 14 collections, 10 minor. Minor pauses 31-36 ms at 420-500 MB live, then 52, 55, 70 ms at 530, 564, 623 MB; one full collection at t=34.6 s, 313 ms; pause total 993 ms. Frames over 25 ms: 10 of 3015 (worst 327 ms, in the full collection). So the 8eae2b1 / idle-backoff work holds on Linux: minors no longer track the allocation rate, but they still creep with the dirty old heap (about 0.2 ms per MB live here), and the major is bounded by the single linked chain. The reproducer dirties nearly every pointerful old block each frame, which is the worst case for per-block dirt tracking; ashui's real flight demo measured 12.8-19.0 ms minors and a 57 ms full collection. Next lever is a precise remembered set (card marking in every tier's stores) or incremental marking; neither is justified by these numbers alone.","files":null}]}